<BaseLayout title="Research methodology — Better Pila" description="How Better Pila finds, verifies, summarizes, and maintains public information.">
  <header class="page-hero"><div class="narrow"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>/</span><span>Methodology</span></div><p class="eyebrow">Evidence before publication</p><h1>How records earn their place here.</h1></div></header>
  <article class="section narrow content paper">
    <h2>Primary sources first</h2><p>Maintainers prefer direct municipal records and national government sources. Secondary reporting may help locate a record but is not normally the only evidence for consequential claims.</p>
    <h2>Human review</h2><p>Every substantive record requires a source and verification date. Drafts remain private until a reviewer checks names, dates, amounts, context, privacy, and neutral wording.</p>
    <h2>Clear uncertainty</h2><p>When sources conflict or information is incomplete, Better Pila describes the limitation rather than guessing. Missing information is not treated as zero.</p>
    <h2>Living records</h2><p>Contacts, current officials, and active projects receive scheduled reviews. Historical and superseded records are archived when they retain public value.</p>
    <h2>Corrections</h2><p>Evidence-backed correction requests are reviewed and material corrections receive a visible note. Administrative revision history is preserved.</p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

